 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the transportation law, in relation to requiring certain\nfor-hire vehicles to install and maintain an interior rear-facing video camera",
    "basePrintNo": "S2379",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2379-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §140, Transp L",
    "lawSection": "Transportation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2379\n\nSPONSOR: PERSAUD\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the transportation law, in relation to requiring certain\nfor-hire vehicles to install and maintain an interior rear-facing video\ncamera\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation requires certain for-hire vehicles to install and main-\ntain an interior rear-facing video camera to bring up-to-date current\nlaw while also adapting to new technology. It would also allow for the\nuse of front facing cameras as long as the minimum requirements are met.\nLastly, this would allow for local law enforcement and local regulatory\nauthority to gain access to the everyday operation recorded on the\ncamera in the for-hire vehicle.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Section 140 of the transportation law is amended by adding a\nnew subdivision 11. 11.a. Requiring all commercial motor vehicles trans-\nport passengers for-hire with a seating capacity of 6 or less, except\nfor a black car, limousine, or luxury limousine, as such terms are\ndefined in section 19 -502 of the administrative code of the city of New\nYork, or as otherwise defined in local law, to install and maintain a\nrear-facing video camera. Such video camera shall be recording at all\ntimes the vehicle is being operated for the purpose of for-hire trans-\nportation, and at minimum shall:\n\n(i) record anyone who enters and exits said vehicle\n\n(ii) store a minimum of the most recent 60 hours of operation and\n\n(iii) have the ability to transmit data or otherwise provide access to\nlocal law enforcement and the local regulatory authority\n\nb. The requirement set forth shall not limit the camera brand or provid-\ner that can be used to comply with this subdivision. The minimum\nrequirement of this subdivision shall suffice.  F. No law shall prohibit\nthe installation and use of a front facing camera as long as the minimum\nrequirement within this subdivision is met. Insurance provider shall be\ngranted similar access to such data.\n\nd. Requirements within the subdivision shall supersede any local regu-\nlations, video cameras installed in commercial motor vehicles engaged in\nthe for-hire transport of passengers.\n\nSection 2. This act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it\nshall have become law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs it stands, current rear facing cameras requirements under 35 RCNY\n59C-01 are outdated and require a court order to access footage. This\nstatewide bill would allow competing providers to sell advanced and\nefficient rear facing cameras at an affordable rate. This bill would\nexempt black cars, limos, and luxury limos from these new requirements.\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021/2022 S186 Referred to Transportation\n\n2019/2020 S6147A Referred to Transportation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
